<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quito</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quito is the capital of Ecuador, set in an Andean basin at about 2,850 metres just south of the equator. Centre of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> and later the northern capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>, it was refounded by the Spanish in 1534 and became the seat of the Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of Quito, famed for its baroque churches and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito-school">Quito School</a> of art. Its junta of 10 August 1809 was among the first moves towards independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quito.jpg" alt="Quito" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asamblea Nacional del Ecuador · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FACHADA_ASAMBLEA_NACIONAL._QUITO,_20_DE_FEBRERO_2020._01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antonio José de Sucre’s victory at the Battle of Pichincha in May 1822 brought in <b>Quito</b>, and Bolívar annexed the port of Guayaquil in July 1822, shortly before meeting José de San Martín there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Two sons claimed the throne: Huáscar, recognised in Cusco, and Atahualpa, backed by the veteran armies in the north around <b>Quito</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Reports of a 'gilded man' who covered himself in gold dust reached the Spanish in <b>Quito</b> in the 1530s, and in July 1539 the treasurer of Sebastián de Belalcázar's expedition testified that it had set out in search of a land called El Dorado.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Quito</b>’s creole elite formed one of the earliest Spanish American juntas on 10 August 1809, but royalist forces suppressed it, and independence came only with the campaign of Antonio José de Sucre, whose victory at the Battle of Pichincha on 24 May 1822 freed Quito.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the wars of independence Viceroy José Fernando de Abascal turned Peru into the royalist base of South America, sending armies that crushed revolutionaries in Upper Peru and <b>Quito</b> and reconquered Chile after the Battle of Rancagua in 1814.</blockquote><a href="/empire/viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Criollo leaders in <b>Quito</b> overthrew the royal president and formed a governing junta, one of the first such moves in Spanish South America.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
